## Proof

**Claim.** The ring $R = M_n(\mathbb{k})$ has the invariant basis number (IBN) property: if $R^m \cong R^p$ as left $R$-modules, then $m = p$.

**Step 1: A Morita equivalence.** Let $e = e_{11} \in R$ be the matrix unit idempotent (1 in the $(1,1)$-entry, 0 elsewhere). The functor
$$F := eR \otimes_R - \;:\; R\text{-Mod} \longrightarrow \mathbb{k}\text{-Mod}$$
is an equivalence of categories (Morita equivalence), with inverse $G = Re \otimes_{\mathbb{k}} -$. Indeed, $eR$ is a progenerator of $R$-Mod and $\operatorname{End}_R(eR) \cong (eRe)^{\mathrm{op}} = \mathbb{k}^{\mathrm{op}} = \mathbb{k}$, so by Morita theory $F$ is an equivalence.

**Step 2: Image of a free module.** Apply $F$ to the free module $R^m$:
$$F(R^m) = eR \otimes_R R^m \cong (eR)^m.$$
As a left $\mathbb{k}$-module (i.e., $\mathbb{k}$-vector space), $eR$ consists of all matrices in $M_n(\mathbb{k})$ with nonzero entries only in the first row, so $\dim_{\mathbb{k}}(eR) = n$. Therefore
$$F(R^m) \cong \mathbb{k}^{nm}, \qquad \dim_{\mathbb{k}} F(R^m) = nm.$$

**Step 3: Conclude IBN.** Suppose $R^m \cong R^p$ as left $R$-modules. Applying the equivalence $F$,
$$\mathbb{k}^{nm} \cong \mathbb{k}^{np} \quad \text{as } \mathbb{k}\text{-vector spaces}.$$
Since $\mathbb{k}$ is a field, vector spaces of different finite dimensions are non-isomorphic, so $nm = np$, and since $n \geq 1$, we obtain $m = p$.

Thus any two finite bases of a finitely generated free left $M_n(\mathbb{k})$-module have the same cardinality. The same argument on the right (or by symmetry of Morita equivalence) gives the right-module version. Hence $M_n(\mathbb{k})$ has the IBN property. $\blacksquare$

